let __v_6564 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"amsterdam\",\"Amsterdam is easy with children: everything is close, the trams take a pram, and NEMO and the science museum are built for them. The bikes are the thing to watch - keep on the pavement side and hold hands at the crossings.\"],[\"athens\",\"Athens is fine with older children and hard work with a toddler: the Acropolis is a stony climb with little shade and the pavements are narrow. Go at opening time, give the afternoon to the National Garden, and take taxis rather than walk the long blocks.\"],[\"bangkok\",\"Bangkok is hot and loud and better with children than people expect: the river boats are the day out, the malls have play areas and aquariums, and taxis are cheap. Traffic is the enemy, so plan around the skytrain and the boats.\"],[\"barcelona\",\"Barcelona is one of the easiest cities in Europe with children: the beach is on the metro, the Gothic Quarter is flat, and the Boqueria is a free hour of noise and fruit. The Sagrada Familia sells out, so book weeks ahead.\"],[\"bath\",\"Bath is a good day out rather than a week: the Roman Baths run a family trail with an audio guide for children, and the baths, the abbey and the river are all within a mile. It is fine with a pram in the centre and steep on the hills out of it.\"],[\"berlin\",\"Berlin works well with children and costs less than most of western Europe: the zoo is one of the best anywhere, the Technikmuseum has trains and planes to climb on, and the parks have playgrounds. It is spread out, so buy a day travel ticket.\"],[\"boston\",\"Boston is a good family city: the Freedom Trail is a walk with stories in it, the harbour and the science museum fill a day, and the T takes a stroller. It is compact and walkable, which is the whole point of it with children.\"],[\"brussels\",\"Brussels is underrated with children: the Grand Place, the comic strip museum and the Atomium are close, and the waffles do a lot of the work. Two days is enough, and it is an easy stop on a longer trip.\"],[\"budapest\",\"Budapest is good with children who like water: the thermal baths take families, and Margaret Island is a park with pools, bikes and a musical fountain. The trams and metro are cheap and frequent.\"],[\"buenos aires\",\"Buenos Aires is a big, late city with children: the parks and the zoo in Palermo are the easy days, and the buses and taxis are cheap. Dinner starts at nine or later, so plan an afternoon snack that counts as their meal.\"],[\"cape town\",\"Cape Town is excellent with children if you have a car: the beaches, the cable car, the penguins at Boulders and the aquarium fill a week. The townships and the mountain roads are not a wander, so plan the drives in daylight.\"],[\"chicago\",\"Chicago is a very good family city: the museums on the lakefront, the beach in summer, and the trains are easy with a stroller. The winter wind off the lake is the reason to go between May and September.\"],[\"copenhagen\",\"Copenhagen is about as good as it gets for small children: Tivoli, the Little Mermaid on a flat seafront walk, and the harbour buses for the price of a metro ticket. It is flat, and prams go everywhere.\"],[\"dubai\",\"Dubai is easy for families and hot for most of the year: the malls have indoor play, aquariums and a ski slope, so it works in August when nothing outdoors does. Anything outdoors belongs to the morning, and the good things need booking.\"],[\"dublin\",\"Dublin is a two-day city with children: the Natural History Museum, the Phoenix Park and the zoo sit close together and cost little. The Guinness tour is not one for them, so split up or skip it.\"],[\"dubrovnik\",\"Dubrovnik is beautiful and hard with a pram: the old town is all steps and the walls are a long, hot walk with no shade. Go at opening time or after five, and take the cable car for the view instead of walking the walls.\"],[\"edinburgh\",\"Edinburgh is a good family city if you pace the hills: the castle is a climb but there is a lift up from the esplanade, the museum is free and built for children, and the Royal Mile is short. In August the festival makes it crowded and expensive.\"],[\"florence\",\"Florence with children is one big sight a day and a lot of ice cream: the Duomo climb suits older ones and the Boboli gardens have space to run. The Uffizi is a hard sell under ten, so take them to the Palazzo Vecchio instead.\"],[\"granada\",\"Granada is worth it with children for the Alhambra alone - book months ahead and go at opening time, because there is little shade. The city is hilly and the tapas come free with a drink, which makes dinner easy and early.\"],[\"hanoi\",\"Hanoi is exciting and full-on with children: the scooters make crossing the road an event, and the old quarter is loud. The water puppet theatre and the parks are the easy wins, and a hotel with a pool earns its money.\"],[\"havana\",\"Havana is a city of old cars, music and heat, and children tend to love the first two: the Malecon, the squares and the museums are cheap. Take the heat seriously and carry water, and expect the food to be plainer than the city suggests.\"],[\"helsinki\",\"Helsinki is calm and easy with children: the ferries to the islands, the zoo on its own island, and playgrounds everywhere in a small, walkable centre. It is expensive, and the winter dark is the reason to go between May and September.\"],[\"istanbul\",\"Istanbul is good with children who can walk: the ferries are the best value day out in the city, and the tram between Sultanahmet and the bazaar takes a pram. The bazaars are crowded and pushy, so go early in the day.\"],[\"krakow\",\"Krakow is a good value family city: the main square is flat and car-free, the dragon under the castle breathes fire every few minutes, and the salt mine is a half day out. The camps nearby are not for young children.\"],[\"kyoto\",\"Kyoto is quiet and beautiful and hard work with small children: the temples ask for quiet and the buses are packed. The monkey park and the bamboo grove suit them, and a stroller is more trouble than it is worth on temple steps.\"],[\"lisbon\",\"Lisbon is good with children if you take the trams and the funicular rather than walk the hills: the castle, the aquarium and Belem fill two easy days. The pavements are slippery when wet, and the hills are real.\"],[\"ljubljana\",\"Ljubljana is a small, gentle city with children: the centre is car-free, the river has cafes on it, and the castle funicular saves the climb. It is a good two-day stop on the way to the lakes or the coast.\"],[\"london\",\"London is one of the best cities in the world with children: free museums, the parks, and a ride on the top deck of a bus is a day out on its own. The tube is not step-free everywhere, so check the station before planning a pram route.\"],[\"los angeles\",\"Los Angeles is a driving city, so plan days out rather than wanders: the beach, the studios and the theme parks are each a half day with a car. Without one it is hard work with children.\"],[\"madrid\",\"Madrid is good with children and cheap to eat: the Retiro has a lake with rowing boats and a playground, and the museums are free in the evenings. Dinner starts late, so plan a proper late-afternoon snack.\"],[\"marrakech\",\"Marrakech is a lot with small children: the souks are hot, crowded and full of scooters, and the food is not always what they will eat. The gardens, the horse-drawn calèche rides and a pool at the hotel make it work.\"],[\"mexico city\",\"Mexico City is huge and rewarding with children: the parks, the anthropology museum and the boats at Xochimilco fill days, and the metro is cheap. It is high up, so take the first day slowly, and eat where it is busy.\"],[\"milan\",\"Milan is a short family stop rather than a holiday: the Duomo, the castle park and the science museum are close, and the trams take a pram. Two days, then on to the lakes.\"],[\"munich\",\"Munich is easy and safe with children: the English Garden, the Deutsches Museum and the beer gardens, where children are welcome and there is usually a playground. The trains out to the lakes and the castles are part of the trip.\"],[\"naples\",\"Naples is loud and fast and children tend to love it: the pizza is cheap, the seafront is a flat walk, and Pompeii and Herculaneum are a short train away. Traffic is the thing to watch and the pavements are narrow.\"],[\"new york\",\"New York is great with children who can keep going: the parks, the Natural History Museum and the Staten Island ferry are cheap days out. Most subway stations have no lift, so plan the stroller around buses and taxis.\"],[\"oslo\",\"Oslo is small, safe and expensive: the ferries, the opera house roof you can walk on, and the sculpture park are the easy days out, and the trams take a pram. A week of self-catering takes the edge off the prices.\"],[\"paris\",\"Paris is good for families if you pick one thing a day: the Luxembourg gardens have boats and a playground, and the big museums are free for under-18s in the EU. Lunch is the easy meal out - restaurants are friendlier at midday than at eight.\"],[\"porto\",\"Porto is a good short family break: the riverfront is flat, the boats and the old tram are the day out, and the port cellars are a walk across the bridge. The climb back up from the river is steep.\"],[\"prague\",\"Prague is good value with children: the castle, the river and the old town are close together, the trams are cheap, and the toy museum is a good wet-afternoon stop. The cobbles are hard on a pram.\"],[\"reykjavik\",\"Reykjavik is a good family base if you drive: the swimming pools are the thing, heated and outdoor and everywhere, and the whales, the glaciers and the golden circle are day trips from it. It is expensive, so an apartment helps.\"],[\"rio de janeiro\",\"Rio is wonderful with children in the right places: the beaches, the cable car up Sugar Loaf and the botanical garden are the easy days. Take advice on which areas to walk and which to take a taxi through, and swim where the locals do.\"],[\"rome\",\"Rome works with children if you keep the days short: the Colosseum and the Forum are one morning, the Pantheon and a gelato another, and the fountains give you free water. The cobbles are hard on a pram and the summer heat is real, so spring or autumn.\"],[\"san francisco\",\"San Francisco is a good family city: the cable cars, the sea lions at Pier 39 and the science museum fill three days, and it is all close. The hills are steep and the summer fog makes it colder than people expect.\"],[\"san sebastian\",\"San Sebastian is a seaside city built for families: the beach curls round the bay in the middle of town, the promenade is flat, and the old town tapas bars take children at the early end of the evening. It is a city for eating and swimming rather than for sights.\"],[\"santorini\",\"Santorini is a honeymoon island more than a family one: the villages are steps and cliff paths with no shade, and the beaches are black stones. With children a resort with a pool on a flatter island works far better.\"],[\"seville\",\"Seville is good with children from March to May: the Alcazar has gardens to run in, the Plaza de Espana has rowing boats, and the tapas are cheap and served early enough for them. July and August are too hot to sightsee with children.\"],[\"singapore\",\"Singapore is one of the easiest cities in Asia with children: it is safe, the metro is step-free and air-conditioned, and the zoo, the aquarium and the gardens fill days. It is expensive, and the heat puts the outdoor plans in the morning.\"],[\"stockholm\",\"Stockholm is lovely with children in summer: the ferries between the islands, the Vasa ship and the open-air museum at Skansen, and prams go everywhere. Winter is dark and cold, so go between May and September.\"],[\"sydney\",\"Sydney is a great family city: the beaches, the ferry to Manly, the zoo on the harbour and the parks, most of it free or cheap. It is spread out, so plan each day around one beach or one ferry.\"],[\"tallinn\",\"Tallinn is a good short family break: the old town is small and walkable, the towers and walls are climbable, and the ferries to Helsinki take two hours. Two days is plenty, and it is cheap by western European standards.\"],[\"tokyo\",\"Tokyo is easier with children than people expect: the trains are safe and punctual, lifts and toilets are everywhere, and there is a small park on every block. The trains are packed at rush hour, so plan around eight in the morning and six at night.\"],[\"toronto\",\"Toronto is a good, manageable family city: the islands by ferry, the aquarium, the science centre and the hockey museum fill days, and the subway takes a stroller. It is clean and English-speaking, which makes the first day easy.\"],[\"valencia\",\"Valencia is a good family city that most people skip: the City of Arts and Sciences has an aquarium and a science museum built for children, and the old town is flat. The beach is a tram ride from the centre.\"],[\"venice\",\"Venice with children is a day of boats and bridges: the water bus is the day out, St Mark's square has pigeons and space, and there are no cars to worry about. A pram is the problem - bridges with steps come every hundred metres, so a carrier is better.\"],[\"vienna\",\"Vienna is quietly excellent with children: the zoo at Schonbrunn, the Prater's old ferris wheel and a playground in every park, with a metro that takes a pram everywhere. The cafes take children, and lunch is cheap at midday.\"],[\"zurich\",\"Zurich is clean, safe and expensive with children: the lake boats, the zoo and the outdoor swimming baths are the days out, and the trams take a pram. A Swiss pass and a supermarket lunch keep the cost down.\"]]}")?;
